    /* benchmarking */
    "bm.hero.title":"No Exact Peer. Three Verified Layers.",
    "bm.hero.sub":"No company anywhere combines aeroponic growing, a woody perennial crop and commercial coffee. Decompose our model, though, and every layer is already validated by companies the market has funded. That gap is the opportunity.",
    "bm.v.kicker":"The Verdict","bm.v.title":"“There Is No Aero-Coffee Company” Is the Point",
    "bm.v.p1":"In the United States — and globally — no company simultaneously operates aeroponic cultivation, a woody perennial crop, and commercial-scale coffee. Asking “who is the comparable?” returns silence for a structural reason: coffee’s biology, cost curve and capital cycle never aligned for indoor growing until now.",
    "bm.v.p2":"Decomposed, every layer of our model is validated by companies the market has already funded:",
    "bm.v.l1t":"Layer 1 — Technology","bm.v.l1d":"Aeroponics and controlled-environment growing: proven by AeroFarms and a vertical-farming industry now in its consolidation era.",
    "bm.v.l2t":"Layer 2 — Business model","bm.v.l2d":"Indoor premium fruit priced as a brand: proven at scale by Oishii’s strawberries.",
    "bm.v.l3t":"Layer 3 — Demand","bm.v.l3d":"Capital appetite for reinventing the coffee supply chain: proven by Atomo Coffee, Suntory and Coca-Cola Japan.",
    "bm.v.close":"Three proven layers, zero companies that combine them. We are not entering a validated red ocean — we have the chance to define the category.",
    "bm.sh.kicker":"Reality Check","bm.sh.title":"The Industry Crashed. That Is Our Best Evidence.",
    "bm.sh.sub":"Between 2023 and 2026, controlled-environment agriculture suffered its defining shakeout. The failures share one pattern — and the survivors share the opposite one.",
    "bm.sh.s1v":"14+","bm.sh.s1l":"CEA companies bankrupted or liquidated, 2023–2026",
    "bm.sh.s2v":"$2B+","bm.sh.s2l":"in venture capital written off in the shakeout",
    "bm.sh.s3v":"0","bm.sh.s3l":"commodity-leaf operators left standing with the old playbook",
    "bm.sh.aero.s":"Chapter 11 (2023) → acquired by Palm Ventures, Apr 2026","bm.sh.aero.l":"The world’s aeroponics pioneer proved the technology — and that commodity microgreens cannot pay for it.",
    "bm.sh.plenty.s":"Chapter 11, Mar 2025","bm.sh.plenty.l":"Re-emerged smaller and pivoted toward premium strawberries — toward high-value crops.",
    "bm.sh.bowery.s":"Shut down, Nov 2024","bm.sh.bowery.l":"Once valued at $2.3B; commodity leafy greens at scale were structurally unprofitable.",
    "bm.sh.acres.s":"Operations ceased, Aug 2026","bm.sh.acres.l":"Filed Chapter 7 within a year of its Soli Organic merger.",
    "bm.sh.freight.s":"Chapter 7, May 2025","bm.sh.freight.l":"Container-farm pioneer acquired by Canada’s Growcer.",
    "bm.st.ch11":"Chapter 11","bm.st.dead":"Closed","bm.st.acq":"Acquired","bm.st.ch7":"Chapter 7",
    "bm.sh.in.t":"The survivors show exactly where value lives",
    "bm.sh.in.p":"Every operator that came through intact — Oishii, Local Bounti, Gotham Greens, and a Plenty that pivoted to strawberries — chose high-value crops, premium pricing, contracted offtake, and operating discipline before expansion. Napell was built on the same doctrine from day one: a premium crop (coffee), brand-grade output, offtake anchored by the Saudi Coffee Company, and a unit-economics-first roadmap.",
    "bm.sh.t1":"High-value crop, not commodity","bm.sh.d1":"Premium specialty coffee instead of undifferentiated leaves.",
    "bm.sh.t2":"Offtake before build-out","bm.sh.d2":"Production anchored to SCC’s licensed 27,000-tonne processing capacity.",
    "bm.sh.t3":"Discipline over burn","bm.sh.d3":"Reinvest from seedling cash flow instead of subsidy-dependent scaling.",
    "bm.sh.t4":"Unit economics first","bm.sh.d4":"$0.97 (¥6.61) cost against a $3.69 (¥25) market price before any expansion decision.",
    "bm.ly.kicker":"Peer Map","bm.ly.title":"Three Comparables Investors Can Verify Today",
    "bm.ly.sub":"Each company below is alive, funded and checkable. Treat them as partial validations of our model — none of them grows coffee, which is exactly the white space we occupy.",
    "bm.ly.o.role":"THE MODEL COMP — indoor premium fruit at scale",
    "bm.ly.o.b1":"Runs the world’s largest indoor vertical strawberry farm; roughly $370M raised, including a $150M Series C first close in May 2026.",
    "bm.ly.o.b2":"Proved that consumers pay brand-grade premiums for premium indoor-grown fruit.",
    "bm.ly.o.b3":"Brought pricing from ~$50 a box down to $4.99–15 as scale arrived — a unit-economics curve we mirror in seedling production.",
    "bm.ly.o.why":"Oishii validated the playbook on strawberries. Napell runs the identical playbook on coffee — a crop with far less indoor supply competition.",
    "bm.ly.a.role":"THE TECH COMP — aeroponics at industrial scale",
    "bm.ly.a.b1":"Decades of aeroponic R&D; the biology and the growing method are real and bankable.",
    "bm.ly.a.b2":"Its 2023 Chapter 11 and the April 2026 Palm Ventures acquisition show commodity output under-prices the technology.",
    "bm.ly.a.b3":"Still holds roughly 70% of the US aeroponic microgreens market after restructuring.",
    "bm.ly.a.why":"AeroFarms proves the growing method; its struggle proves crop choice matters more. Coffee’s pricing power is the difference.",
    "bm.ly.at.role":"THE DEMAND COMP — capital loves coffee reinvention",
    "bm.ly.at.b1":"~$61M raised for beanless coffee: Suntory invested in 2023, a $7.8M Series B closed Jan 2025, and Coca-Cola Japan entered in June 2026.",
    "bm.ly.at.b2":"Confirms deep investor appetite for fixing coffee’s fragile supply chain.",
    "bm.ly.at.b3":"Operates upstream of the bean; Napell instead secures the plant itself — the scarcest layer.",
    "bm.ly.at.why":"Atomo proves money flows to coffee disruption. Compound Foods ($4.5M seed, quiet since) shows execution — not theme — separates winners.",
    "bm.t.kicker":"At a Glance","bm.t.h1":"Company","bm.t.h2":"Layer proven","bm.t.h3":"Crop & model","bm.t.h4":"Capital signal","bm.t.h5":"Napell difference",
    "bm.t.r1.c2":"Premium-fruit business model","bm.t.r1.c3":"Strawberries · indoor · brand-priced","bm.t.r1.c4":"~$370M across four rounds","bm.t.r1.c5":"Coffee is a larger, scarcer prize",
    "bm.t.r2.c2":"Aeroponic technology","bm.t.r2.c3":"Leafy greens · aeroponics","bm.t.r2.c4":"Chapter 11 → acquired 2026","bm.t.r2.c5":"We pair the method with a premium crop",
    "bm.t.r3.c2":"Coffee-demand innovation","bm.t.r3.c3":"Beanless coffee · alt ingredients","bm.t.r3.c4":"~$61M · Suntory · Coca-Cola Japan","bm.t.r3.c5":"We secure the plant, not a substitute",
    "bm.t.r4.c2":"Coffee-adjacent theme","bm.t.r4.c3":"Fermentation-based coffee","bm.t.r4.c4":"~$4.5M seed only","bm.t.r4.c5":"Execution gap shows the barrier is real",
    "bm.wh.kicker":"Why the Gap","bm.wh.title":"Four Structural Reasons Nobody Combined It",
    "bm.wh.sub":"The absence of a comparable is not a demand problem — it is a timing problem. Each barrier is now breaking.",
    "bm.wh.r1.t":"Coffee is a woody perennial","bm.wh.r1.d":"Multi-year payback cycles repelled venture capital built for quick, standardisable leafy greens.",
    "bm.wh.r2.t":"Indoor cost vs. origin cost","bm.wh.r2.d":"Indoor farming could not compete with Brazil, Vietnam or Colombia on commercial-grade beans.",
    "bm.wh.r3.t":"No local growing tradition","bm.wh.r3.d":"Little coffee germplasm or agronomic data existed to build on — until tissue culture made it programmable.",
